pthreads: a new thread loads the program's a4 in a base-relative build

CreateNewProc starts the new process at StarterFunc with whatever a4 exec
leaves, so in a program built -fbaserel or -fbaserel32 every global the
thread read (SysBase first) came from the wrong place, and the program hung
as soon as it started a thread. Under __baserel__, StarterFunc now calls the
startup code's geta4 first and then runs the body, a separate function, so
nothing reads data before a4 is right. A plain build is unchanged.

On daletop, t3_threads built -fbaserel32 and -fbaserel (libnix 0007 and a
base-relative libos32compat) prints "t3 OK" and returns 0; before, both hung.

